204
00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:52.030
<v Speaker 1>It exists entirely by virtue of the divine reality it projects.

205
00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:54.590
<v Speaker 1>The world is God making himself apparent.

206
00:11:54.670 --> 00:11:58.110
<v Speaker 2>The distinction between the apparent and the hidden is crucial here.

207
00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:04.190
<v Speaker 2>If creation is the face of the apparent, it means the physical world is not competing with God for reality.

208
00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:05.550
<v Speaker 1>Yes, that's a key distinction.

209
00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:08.830
<v Speaker 2>It is not an alien substance that God has to fight against or push through.

210
00:12:09.310 --> 00:12:12.990
<v Speaker 2>The world is the outward projection of God's own desire to be known.

211
00:12:13.150 --> 00:12:17.790
<v Speaker 1>We can visualize this with a second analogy, one that moves from the page to the sky.

212
00:12:18.910 --> 00:12:22.590
<v Speaker 1>Think of light streaming out from a fiercely luminous source.

213
00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:23.390
<v Speaker 2>Like the sun?

214
00:12:23.470 --> 00:12:24.110
<v Speaker 1>Like the sun.

215
00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:26.390
<v Speaker 1>The creation is like that pouring light.

216
00:12:26.990 --> 00:12:30.590
<v Speaker 1>Imagine a single beam of light breaking into a dusty room.

217
00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:34.670
<v Speaker 1>That light perfectly discloses the source.

218
00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:35.790
<v Speaker 2>It tells you everything.

219
00:12:35.790 --> 00:12:39.870
<v Speaker 1>It tells you everything about the radiance, the warmth, the brilliance of the source.

220
00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:41.550
<v Speaker 1>It is the evidence of the source.

221
00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:43.030
<v Speaker 1>But here is the critical part.

222
00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:47.310
<v Speaker 1>It does this without ever emptying or diminishing the source.

223
00:12:47.830 --> 00:12:50.830
<v Speaker 1>The sun does not become less bright because it illuminates room.

224
00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:54.350
<v Speaker 2>The hidden remains the inexhaustible, uncreated depth.

225
00:12:54.590 --> 00:12:55.550
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

226
00:12:55.710 --> 00:13:00.510
<v Speaker 1>The hidden is the core that never diminishes no matter how much light is poured outward into the universe.

227
00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:07.830
<v Speaker 1>The hidden is the essence of God, completely beyond human comprehension, completely untouched by the limitations of time and space.

228
00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:09.550
<v Speaker 2>So the apparent is the projection.

229
00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:13.150
<v Speaker 1>The apparent is the outward projection of that light into the theater of existence.

230
00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:14.670
<v Speaker 1>The light we see is the apparent.

231
00:13:14.830 --> 00:13:17.550
<v Speaker 1>The blinding core we cannot look directly at is the hidden.

232
00:13:17.710 --> 00:13:22.590
<v Speaker 2>This brings us directly to the scriptural foundation, which ties this all together perfectly.

233
00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:25.710
<v Speaker 2>These 2 names are not just philosophical concepts.

234
00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:28.270
<v Speaker 2>They are spoken in a single breath in the Quran.

235
00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:29.070
<v Speaker 1>Yes, they are.

236
00:13:29.150 --> 00:13:31.230
<v Speaker 2>We read in chapter 57, verse 3.

237
00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:36.670
<v Speaker 2>He is the first and the last, the outward and the inward, and he has knowledge of all things.

238
00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:39.630
<v Speaker 1>The outward and the inward, the apparent and the hidden.

239
00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:43.390
<v Speaker 2>Holding those dualities together is the only way the world makes sense.

240
00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:52.910
<v Speaker 2>He's the outward, the apparent, meaning the visible universe, the heavy, dense, opaque world we struggle with, is actually his self-disclosure.

241
00:13:52.990 --> 00:13:54.590
<v Speaker 1>It is the light hitting the dust.

242
00:13:54.590 --> 00:14:00.110
<v Speaker 2>And he is the inward, the hidden, meaning his absolute essence remains forever beyond our grasp.

243
00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:06.590
<v Speaker 2>If we split those two realities, if we only focus on the outward without recognizing it as a projection of the inward,

244
00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:08.750
<v Speaker 2>The world becomes an opaque wall again.

245
00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:10.670
<v Speaker 2>It becomes just a room full of dust.

246
00:14:10.670 --> 00:14:16.750
<v Speaker 1>This exact pairing of the outward and the inward became the absolute hinge for an entire tradition of reading the world.

247
00:14:17.230 --> 00:14:24.910
<v Speaker 1>We can look to the Fatimid poet, a towering figure who taught that every outward form in existence is essentially a husk enclosing an inner reality.

248
00:14:24.990 --> 00:14:26.190
<v Speaker 2>The husk and the kernel.

249
00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:26.590
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

250
00:14:26.990 --> 00:14:34.830
<v Speaker 1>Whether it is a verse of scripture, a physical object in nature, or an event in your own life, it possesses an outward surface and an inward depth.

251
00:14:35.470 --> 00:14:44.910
<v Speaker 1>To move from the letter to the meaning, to move from the husk to the kernel, the intellect must engage in a practice historically called tawil, the inner interpretation.

252
00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:48.030
<v Speaker 1>It is a rigorous movement of the mind and the heart.

253
00:14:48.270 --> 00:14:51.150
<v Speaker 1>The intellect's task is not to destroy the husk.

254
00:14:51.550 --> 00:14:53.470
<v Speaker 1>You do not smash the physical world.

255
00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:56.670
<v Speaker 1>You do not hate the husk because the husk protects the kernel.

256
00:14:57.070 --> 00:14:59.070
<v Speaker 2>Just like you don't scrape the ink off the page.

257
00:14:59.070 --> 00:14:59.790
<v Speaker 1>Precisely.

258
00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:04.750
<v Speaker 1>Instead, you use the inner interpretation to crack the husk open to reach the meaning.

259
00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:06.910
<v Speaker 1>It means taking a thing back to its origin.

260
00:15:07.070 --> 00:15:08.830
<v Speaker 1>You trace the light back to the source.

261
00:15:09.070 --> 00:15:11.150
<v Speaker 1>You look at the physical limitation of the object.

262
00:15:11.390 --> 00:15:17.390
<v Speaker 1>You recognize it as a word spoken by the apparent, and you follow that word back to the hidden silence from which it emerged.

263
00:15:17.630 --> 00:15:21.790
<v Speaker 2>That inner interpretation is a profound shift in how we live.

264
00:15:22.870 --> 00:15:24.430
<v Speaker 2>reversal of the outward projection.

265
00:15:24.430 --> 00:15:25.110
<v Speaker 1>Beautifully said.

266
00:15:25.310 --> 00:15:32.990
<v Speaker 2>If creation is God speaking a word, the inner interpretation is the human being hearing that word and tracing the sound back to the silence.

267
00:15:33.790 --> 00:15:36.350
<v Speaker 2>It requires a fundamental shift in perception.

268
00:15:36.750 --> 00:15:43.070
<v Speaker 2>It means looking at the heavy, demanding world not as a series of obstacles, but as a series of invitations.

269
00:15:43.310 --> 00:15:47.710
<v Speaker 1>And no one pressed this mystery harder or with more beautiful provocation than the Aphorist.

270
00:15:47.950 --> 00:15:55.550
<v Speaker 1>He meditates on this mystery through a cascade of rhetorical questions that challenge our basic, lazy assumptions about what a veil even is.

271
00:15:55.550 --> 00:15:57.310
<v Speaker 2>He really tears down those illusions.

272
00:15:57.350 --> 00:15:58.230
<v Speaker 2>What does he ask?

273
00:15:58.230 --> 00:16:03.670
<v Speaker 1>He strips away the illusion of the veil layer by layer simply by asking the mind to consider the logical

274
00:16:03.990 --> 00:16:05.870
<v Speaker 1>of a creation hiding its creator.

275
00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:09.710
<v Speaker 1>He asks, how can something veil him when he manifests everything?

276
00:16:09.870 --> 00:16:10.430
<v Speaker 1>Think about that.

277
00:16:10.430 --> 00:16:11.630
<v Speaker 1>That's A staggering thought.

278
00:16:11.870 --> 00:16:15.310
<v Speaker 1>If God is the light by which you see the veil, how can the veil hide him?

279
00:16:15.470 --> 00:16:16.110
<v Speaker 1>He goes on.

280
00:16:16.350 --> 00:16:19.950
<v Speaker 1>How can a veil exist when he was manifest before the existence of everything?

281
00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:21.790
<v Speaker 2>The timing alone shatters the idea.

282
00:16:21.790 --> 00:16:22.430
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

283
00:16:22.430 --> 00:16:29.390
<v Speaker 1>How can a temporary limited thing hide the eternal limitless source that sustains its very existence in that exact moment?

284
00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:33.230
<v Speaker 2>The sheer wonder of the contingent world standing alongside

285
00:16:33.910 --> 00:16:37.390
<v Speaker 2>eternity is staggering when you actually pause to observe it.

286
00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:44.190
<v Speaker 2>The aphorist forces us to realize that seeing the veil at all means you are already seeing by his light.

287
00:16:44.430 --> 00:16:46.350
<v Speaker 1>Contingency is a great word here.

288
00:16:46.590 --> 00:16:48.190
<v Speaker 1>The world is contingent.

289
00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:50.670
<v Speaker 1>It relies entirely on something else to exist.

290
00:16:50.670 --> 00:16:52.110
<v Speaker 1>It cannot hold itself up.

291
00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:52.670
<v Speaker 2>Right.

292
00:16:52.750 --> 00:17:01.790
<v Speaker 2>The fact that you can perceive a boundary, the fact that you can perceive A limitation, a tree, a stone, a letter on a page, means you are perceiving the divine act of limitation.

293
00:17:01.870 --> 00:17:03.550
<v Speaker 1>Yes, the very act itself.

294
00:17:03.910 --> 00:17:08.270
<v Speaker 2>Existence of the veil is the undeniable proof of the power that manifested it.

295
00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:10.190
<v Speaker 2>A shadow proves the light.

296
00:17:10.670 --> 00:17:17.310
<v Speaker 2>So the contingent world, this temporary fragile world of things that we bump into every day, is held in existence alongside the eternal.

297
00:17:17.630 --> 00:17:18.910
<v Speaker 2>It doesn't block the eternal.

298
00:17:18.990 --> 00:17:20.270
<v Speaker 2>It is suspended within it.

299
00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:21.150
<v Speaker 2>is a marvel.

300
00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:23.470
<v Speaker 1>But the mind struggles with this marvel.

301
00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:24.590
<v Speaker 1>It is dizzying.

302
00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:29.470
<v Speaker 1>The rational mind, which likes neat categories and solid walls, rebels against this.

303
00:17:29.750 --> 00:17:33.230
<v Speaker 1>And that leads to a necessary challenge, a real friction we have to address.

304
00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:37.710
<v Speaker 2>And I am going to push back again because my rational mind is rebelling right now.

305
00:17:38.190 --> 00:17:39.470
<v Speaker 2>That sounds like wordplay.

306
00:17:39.550 --> 00:17:40.030
<v Speaker 1>Does it?

307
00:17:40.430 --> 00:17:41.630
<v Speaker 2>I am sorry, but it does.

308
00:17:42.110 --> 00:17:47.510
<v Speaker 2>If everything reveals God, if the ink is the revelation of the author, what does the word veil?

309
00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:48.730
<v Speaker 2>even mean anymore?

310
00:17:49.370 --> 00:17:57.050
<v Speaker 2>If there is no hidden God behind the curtain, if the curtain itself is the revelation, aren't we just equating the world with God?

311
00:17:57.290 --> 00:17:58.650
<v Speaker 1>That is the danger.

312
00:17:58.650 --> 00:18:00.170
<v Speaker 1>That is the cliff edge we are walking on.

313
00:18:00.170 --> 00:18:01.770
<v Speaker 2>It's a massive cliff.

314
00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:11.290
<v Speaker 2>I mean, if every tree, every star, every stone, every physical object is a manifestation of the apparent, aren't we dangerously close to saying the universe itself is divine?

315
00:18:11.570 --> 00:18:15.530
<v Speaker 2>which entirely collapses the very distinction between the creator and the created.

316
00:18:15.770 --> 00:18:19.530
<v Speaker 2>If the tree is God making himself apparent, am I supposed to worship the tree?

317
00:18:19.530 --> 00:18:22.970
<v Speaker 1>That is the most important danger to address in this entire deep dive.

318
00:18:23.210 --> 00:18:28.970
<v Speaker 1>It is the exact trap that many spiritual seekers fall into when they first encounter this profound nearness.

319
00:18:29.290 --> 00:18:32.250
<v Speaker 1>And it brings us to the foundational sentence from the imam's teaching.

320
00:18:32.370 --> 00:18:33.690
<v Speaker 2>What is the guardrail here?

321
00:18:33.850 --> 00:18:38.330
<v Speaker 1>This specific logic protects the entire vision from collapsing into pantheism.

322
00:18:38.810 --> 00:18:40.250
<v Speaker 1>It is the ultimate guardrail.

323
00:18:40.570 --> 00:18:45.770
<v Speaker 1>The imam states, he is with everything but not in physical nearness.

324
00:18:46.170 --> 00:18:49.370
<v Speaker 1>And he is other than everything but not in physical separation.

325
00:18:49.370 --> 00:18:51.890
<v Speaker 2>That is a dense, incredibly tightly wound sentence.

326
00:18:51.890 --> 00:18:53.370
<v Speaker 2>Let's examine the mechanics of that carefully.

327
00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:54.410
<v Speaker 2>Let's take the first half.

328
00:18:54.730 --> 00:18:57.530
<v Speaker 2>He is with everything but not in physical nearness.

329
00:18:57.610 --> 00:18:58.090
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

330
00:18:58.330 --> 00:19:00.730
<v Speaker 1>This establishes imminence, presence.

331
00:19:00.890 --> 00:19:04.970
<v Speaker 1>God is intimately present with the tree, with the stone, with the ink on the page.

332
00:19:05.050 --> 00:19:05.770
<v Speaker 2>The withness.

333
00:19:06.010 --> 00:19:08.970
<v Speaker 1>But, and this is the God rail, not in physical nearness.

334
00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:11.610
<v Speaker 1>He is not mixed into the material of the world.

335
00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:16.650
<v Speaker 1>He's not a physical substance blending with another physical substance, like sugar dissolving in water.

336
00:19:17.290 --> 00:19:19.450
<v Speaker 1>He is with the tree, but he is not the wood.

337
00:19:19.610 --> 00:19:22.250
<v Speaker 1>He is with the ink, but he is not the soot.

338
00:19:22.810 --> 00:19:23.690
<v Speaker 2>And the second-half.

339
00:19:24.010 --> 00:19:27.130
<v Speaker 2>He is other than everything, but not in physical separation.

340
00:19:27.610 --> 00:19:30.570
<v Speaker 1>This establishes transcendence, distinctness.

341
00:19:30.970 --> 00:19:34.010
<v Speaker 1>God is absolutely, completely other than the tree.

342
00:19:34.010 --> 00:19:34.730
<v Speaker 1>He is uncreated.

343
00:19:34.730 --> 00:19:35.690
<v Speaker 1>The tree is created.

344
00:19:35.690 --> 00:19:36.370
<v Speaker 1>He is infinite.

345
00:19:36.370 --> 00:19:37.210
<v Speaker 1>The tree is finite.

346
00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:38.970
<v Speaker 2>So he isn't the object itself.

347
00:19:39.130 --> 00:19:43.290
<v Speaker 1>But, and here's the other guardrail, not in physical separation.

348
00:19:43.850 --> 00:19:46.970
<v Speaker 1>He is not pushed away into a distant galaxy.

349
00:19:47.210 --> 00:19:50.410
<v Speaker 1>He is not sitting on a cloud a billion light years away.

350
00:19:50.650 --> 00:19:53.530
<v Speaker 1>He is distinctly other, but intimately near.

351
00:19:54.090 --> 00:20:01.770
<v Speaker 2>This brings us to the stakes of getting this wrong, because if you lean too far toward either half of that sentence, the whole spiritual architecture collapses.

352
00:20:01.930 --> 00:20:02.530
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

353
00:20:02.530 --> 00:20:06.650
<v Speaker 1>And the metaphysician explains the severe stakes of maintaining this balance.

354
00:20:07.210 --> 00:20:15.450
<v Speaker 1>There are two fatal errors on the spiritual path, 2 traps that the human mind constantly falls into because we struggle to hold these two truths together.

355
00:20:15.690 --> 00:20:18.010
<v Speaker 1>The first trap is the error of collapse.

356
00:20:18.050 --> 00:20:19.690
<v Speaker 2>Which leads to idolatry.

357
00:20:19.690 --> 00:20:22.090
<v Speaker 1>It is the absolute root of idolatry.

358
00:20:22.410 --> 00:20:28.410
<v Speaker 1>This happens when you stress God's nearness, his imminence so much that the creator and the creature dissolve into one another.

359
00:20:28.650 --> 00:20:32.490
<v Speaker 1>You blur the line, you make God finite, you trap him in a physical boundary.

360
00:20:32.490 --> 00:20:34.090
<v Speaker 2>You start worshiping the objects.

361
00:20:34.090 --> 00:20:37.090
<v Speaker 1>You look at the beauty of the light hitting the dust and you worship the dust.

362
00:20:37.370 --> 00:20:38.570
<v Speaker 1>the ink for the author.

363
00:20:38.730 --> 00:20:41.130
<v Speaker 2>The error of collapse is deeply tempting.

364
00:20:41.770 --> 00:20:44.010
<v Speaker 2>We have to acknowledge why humans do this.

365
00:20:44.490 --> 00:20:46.730
<v Speaker 2>We want something tangible to hold onto.

366
00:20:48.210 --> 00:20:50.010
<v Speaker 2>Our bodies crave physical touch.

367
00:20:50.010 --> 00:20:51.850
<v Speaker 2>Our eyes crave physical beauty.

368
00:20:51.850 --> 00:20:53.290
<v Speaker 1>It's natural to want that.

369
00:20:53.370 --> 00:20:58.730
<v Speaker 2>We want a God we can measure, a God we can touch, a God we can put in our pocket or build a shrine around.

370
00:20:59.050 --> 00:21:01.930
<v Speaker 2>But the moment we do that, we have lost the infinite.

371
00:21:02.250 --> 00:21:05.210
<v Speaker 2>We have trapped the limitless in a cage of our own making.

372
00:21:05.290 --> 00:21:07.370
<v Speaker 1>That is the idolatry of the ancient world.

373
00:21:07.370 --> 00:21:13.690
<v Speaker 1>But it is also the idolatry of the modern mind when we worship nature or human intellect or the physical universe itself.

374
00:21:13.850 --> 00:21:14.970
<v Speaker 2>So what is the second trap?

375
00:21:15.290 --> 00:21:17.130
<v Speaker 1>The second trap is the error of exile.

376
00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:25.050
<v Speaker 1>This happens when you stress God's transcendence so much, you stress his otherness so much, that God is pushed entirely out of the world.

377
00:21:25.290 --> 00:21:26.330
<v Speaker 2>A total separation.

378
00:21:26.330 --> 00:21:33.450
<v Speaker 1>You emphasize his incomparability to such an extreme degree that you leave behind a mute, empty, mechanical universe.

379
00:21:33.450 --> 00:21:39.610
<v Speaker 1>You say, God is so holy, so distant, so pure, that he has nothing to do with this dirty physical world.

380
00:21:39.650 --> 00:21:41.050
<v Speaker 2>And the result of that is devastating.

381
00:21:41.210 --> 00:21:42.970
<v Speaker 1>It is absolute isolation.

382
00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:45.210
<v Speaker 1>The world is stripped of its signs.

383
00:21:45.690 --> 00:21:47.610
<v Speaker 1>The ink on the page is no longer

384
00:21:47.690 --> 00:21:48.810
<v Speaker 1>letter from a loved one.

385
00:21:48.970 --> 00:21:52.170
<v Speaker 1>It is just random chemical marks in a chaotic universe.

386
00:21:52.570 --> 00:21:59.530
<v Speaker 1>Humanity is left isolated in a mechanical void, overseen by an absent clockmaker who wound the universe up and walked away.

387
00:22:00.010 --> 00:22:02.170
<v Speaker 2>Okay, I have to concede the logic here.

388
00:22:02.570 --> 00:22:08.970
<v Speaker 2>I was pushing back hard against the idea that the veil is the revelation, fearing it would lead to worshiping the world.

389
00:22:09.570 --> 00:22:15.610
<v Speaker 2>But I see the guardrail now, and it hinges entirely on grasping the non-numerical oneness of God.

390
00:22:15.930 --> 00:22:16.850
<v Speaker 1>Let's explore that.

391
00:22:16.850 --> 00:22:18.890
<v Speaker 1>What do you mean by non-numerical oneness?

392
00:22:18.970 --> 00:22:22.170
<v Speaker 2>Well, this is the crucial distinction that solves my earlier frustration.

393
00:22:22.570 --> 00:22:32.250
<v Speaker 2>If God were simply one in the numerical sense, like one apple sitting on a table or one king ruling a kingdom, then the existence of the universe would mean there are now two things.

394
00:22:33.050 --> 00:22:34.010
<v Speaker 2>God and the universe.

395
00:22:34.090 --> 00:22:36.450
<v Speaker 1>Right, apple and orange, king and subjects.

396
00:22:36.450 --> 00:22:36.930
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

397
00:22:36.930 --> 00:22:39.530
<v Speaker 2>There would be two distinct objects sitting side by side.

398
00:22:39.770 --> 00:22:42.810
<v Speaker 2>There would be rival realities competing for space and existence.

399
00:22:43.130 --> 00:22:45.210
<v Speaker 2>If the universe takes up space, it pushes God out.

400
00:22:45.770 --> 00:22:48.330
<v Speaker 2>If God takes up space, he crushes the universe.

401
00:22:48.410 --> 00:22:49.690
<v Speaker 1>That's the spatial dilemma.

402
00:22:49.930 --> 00:22:57.610
<v Speaker 2>But because God is infinite, because his oneness is absolute and non-numerical, the created world cannot be a rival reality.

403
00:22:58.210 --> 00:23:00.490
<v Speaker 2>Infinity plus one is still infinity.

404
00:23:00.650 --> 00:23:03.370
<v Speaker 1>That is such an important mathematical analogy.

405
00:23:03.450 --> 00:23:08.570
<v Speaker 2>The existence of the contingent world doesn't add to God and it doesn't subtract from him.

406
00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:13.770
<v Speaker 2>It's apparent otherness isn't a literal physical wall shutting God out.

407
00:23:14.090 --> 00:23:15.930
<v Speaker 2>It is simply his self showing.

408
00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:18.090
<v Speaker 2>The veil only becomes opaque.

409
00:23:18.250 --> 00:23:24.890
<v Speaker 2>It only becomes a dark, frustrating barrier when we fail to hold his imminence and his transcendence together in our minds.

410
00:23:24.970 --> 00:23:25.850
<v Speaker 1>Beautifully said.

411
00:23:25.930 --> 00:23:32.250
<v Speaker 1>To hold those opposites together, to see the ink and hear the author simultaneously is the very definition of spiritual vision.

412
00:23:32.250 --> 00:23:33.450
<v Speaker 2>It is the ultimate goal.

413
00:23:33.610 --> 00:23:36.410
<v Speaker 1>The early Sufi was once asked a profound question.

414
00:23:36.490 --> 00:23:41.210
<v Speaker 1>He was asked how he came to know God, and his answer cuts right to the heart of everything we were discussing.

415
00:23:41.690 --> 00:23:44.690
<v Speaker 1>He answered through the fact that he brings opposites together.

416
00:23:44.690 --> 00:23:45.970
<v Speaker 2>Through the holding together of contradiction.

417
00:23:46.170 --> 00:23:46.810
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

418
00:23:47.130 --> 00:23:50.850
<v Speaker 1>The polished heart can hold together what the rational mind must tear apart.

419
00:23:51.290 --> 00:23:57.130
<v Speaker 1>The rational mind, governed by logic and geometry, looks at transcendence and immanence and sees a flat contradiction.

420
00:23:57.290 --> 00:23:59.450
<v Speaker 2>It sees a paradox that cannot be solved.

421
00:23:59.450 --> 00:24:01.930
<v Speaker 1>It says you cannot be far and near at the same time.

422
00:24:02.530 --> 00:24:09.050
<v Speaker 1>But the polished heart looks at those same 2 realities and sees the single breathing reality of existence.

423
00:24:09.490 --> 00:24:11.290
<v Speaker 1>It is not a logical demonstration.

424
00:24:11.450 --> 00:24:13.210
<v Speaker 1>It is a direct witnessing.

425
00:24:13.450 --> 00:24:16.090
<v Speaker 2>But here's where we hit a massive practical problem.

426
00:24:16.970 --> 00:24:26.890
<v Speaker 2>All of this metaphysical vision, this beautiful soaring architecture of thought, but the apparent and the hidden, the ink and the author, the light and the dust.

427
00:24:27.930 --> 00:24:30.730
<v Speaker 2>It is completely useless if it remains an idle theory.

428
00:24:30.730 --> 00:24:32.490
<v Speaker 1>You're getting to the core of the issue.

429
00:24:32.570 --> 00:24:39.370
<v Speaker 2>It is a wonderful philosophical edifice to marvel at, but how does the listener actually polish the heart to see this way?

430
00:24:39.450 --> 00:24:41.290
<v Speaker 2>Because right now, my heart is not polished.

431
00:24:41.290 --> 00:24:43.530
<v Speaker 2>I still get frustrated by the heavy, opaque world.

432
00:24:43.610 --> 00:24:44.330
<v Speaker 1>We all do.

433
00:24:44.490 --> 00:24:48.250
<v Speaker 2>The eye does not cure its own blindness just by understanding the theory of light.

434
00:24:48.690 --> 00:24:50.650
<v Speaker 2>What is the mechanism of transformation?

435
00:24:50.650 --> 00:24:52.250
<v Speaker 1>This is the most vital question.

436
00:24:52.330 --> 00:24:56.810
<v Speaker 1>We have to move from theory to practice, from philosophy to the quintessence of worship.

437
00:24:57.770 --> 00:25:00.170
<v Speaker 1>we are talking about is not granted by reading a book.

438
00:25:00.650 --> 00:25:03.970
<v Speaker 1>It is grown only in a specific, grueling discipline.

439
00:25:03.970 --> 00:25:05.050
<v Speaker 2>A discipline of the heart.

440
00:25:05.210 --> 00:25:05.690
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

441
00:25:06.090 --> 00:25:11.850
<v Speaker 1>And the scriptural commands in the Quran regarding this discipline are incredibly explicit and incredibly frequent.

442
00:25:12.250 --> 00:25:18.170
<v Speaker 1>In chapter 76, verse 25, we are told, invoke the name of your Lord morning and evening.

443
00:25:18.250 --> 00:25:20.090
<v Speaker 2>That sets a daily rhythm.

444
00:25:20.090 --> 00:25:25.850
<v Speaker 1>In chapter 33, verse 41, the command is, invoke God with much invocation.

445
00:25:26.090 --> 00:25:27.650
<v Speaker 1>And in chapter 7, verse 205,

446
00:25:27.770 --> 00:25:28.970
<v Speaker 1>We are instructed.

447
00:25:29.210 --> 00:25:32.410
<v Speaker 1>Invoke your Lord within yourself, beneath your breath.

448
00:25:32.650 --> 00:25:34.090
<v Speaker 2>The emphasis there is incredible.

449
00:25:34.410 --> 00:25:39.450
<v Speaker 1>The key operating word running like a steel thread through all of these commands is abundance.

450
00:25:39.770 --> 00:25:42.730
<v Speaker 1>It is not a suggestion for occasional polite reflection.

451
00:25:42.810 --> 00:25:45.930
<v Speaker 1>It is a demand for continuous abundant invocation.

452
00:25:45.930 --> 00:25:50.490
<v Speaker 2>The requirement of abundance is so revealing because it provides A diagnosis of the human condition.

453
00:25:51.450 --> 00:25:52.930
<v Speaker 2>Why must the invocation be abundant?

454
00:25:52.930 --> 00:25:56.370
<v Speaker 2>Why isn't it enough to just think about God once a week or once a day?

455
00:25:56.370 --> 00:25:57.450
<v Speaker 1>What's the diagnosis?

456
00:25:57.450 --> 00:26:02.810
<v Speaker 2>Because the natural default state of the human mind as it wanders through the heavy physical world is forgetfulness.

457
00:26:03.290 --> 00:26:04.490
<v Speaker 2>Spiritual amnesia.

458
00:26:04.730 --> 00:26:08.090
<v Speaker 2>We are constantly gravitatingly drawn back to the surfaces.

459
00:26:08.330 --> 00:26:11.210
<v Speaker 2>We are drawn back to the weight, the color, and the stress of the veil.

460
00:26:11.370 --> 00:26:15.850
<v Speaker 1>Which brings us to our third analogy, and it perfectly captures this diagnosis.

461
00:26:16.530 --> 00:26:22.410
<v Speaker 1>The heart forgets as a matter of second nature, just as an untended blade inevitably gathers rust.

462
00:26:22.610 --> 00:26:24.570
<v Speaker 2>Let's dive deep into that image.

463
00:26:24.730 --> 00:26:26.890
<v Speaker 1>Think of a blacksmith forging a blade.

464
00:26:27.530 --> 00:26:32.170
<v Speaker 1>He heats the metal, he strikes it, he polishes it until it shines like a mirror.

465
00:26:32.410 --> 00:26:34.330
<v Speaker 1>It can reflect the sun perfectly.

466
00:26:34.490 --> 00:26:35.650
<v Speaker 2>A flawless surface.

467
00:26:35.650 --> 00:26:41.930
<v Speaker 1>But if you take that highly polished sword and leave it exposed to the open air, the oxidation process begins immediately.

468
00:26:42.170 --> 00:26:43.050
<v Speaker 1>It doesn't wait.

469
00:26:43.050 --> 00:26:47.050
<v Speaker 1>The oxygen in the air begins to bond with the iron, creating rust.

470
00:26:47.130 --> 00:26:50.490
<v Speaker 2>And a medicine taken once does not cure a chronic condition.

471
00:26:50.570 --> 00:26:51.290
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

472
00:26:51.370 --> 00:26:53.850
<v Speaker 1>Spiritual amnesia is our chronic condition.

473
00:26:54.170 --> 00:26:56.890
<v Speaker 1>The physical world is the air that oxidizes the heart.

474
00:26:57.410 --> 00:27:02.890
<v Speaker 1>The heavy, demanding, opaque nature of daily life is constantly rusting our spiritual vision.

475
00:27:02.890 --> 00:27:04.410
<v Speaker 2>So we need continuous polishing.

476
00:27:04.490 --> 00:27:09.770
<v Speaker 1>In vacation, the remembrance of the name must be continual because the rusting of the heart is continual.

477
00:27:09.970 --> 00:27:13.050
<v Speaker 1>The moment you stop polishing the blade, the rust begins to form.

478
00:27:13.210 --> 00:27:19.130
<v Speaker 1>You cannot polish the sword on a Friday and expect it to shine brightly the following Thursday if you have left it out in the rain.

479
00:27:19.370 --> 00:27:25.850
<v Speaker 2>The physical reality of rust is such a hauntingly perfect image for the creeping opacity of the world.

480
00:27:26.330 --> 00:27:28.010
<v Speaker 2>Because rust doesn't happen all at once.

481
00:27:28.010 --> 00:27:28.970
<v Speaker 1>It's gradual.

482
00:27:29.050 --> 00:27:32.490
<v Speaker 2>A polished blade doesn't turn brown and flaky in a single second.

483
00:27:32.970 --> 00:27:35.930
<v Speaker 2>It loses its gleam slowly, imperceptibly.

484
00:27:36.410 --> 00:27:40.330
<v Speaker 2>First it just looks slightly dull, then a small spot appears, then another.

485
00:27:40.970 --> 00:27:46.170
<v Speaker 2>Until one day, it is entirely coated in rust and it can no longer reflect the sun at all.

486
00:27:46.730 --> 00:27:50.570
<v Speaker 2>The continuous invocation is the only friction that keeps the metal clear.

487
00:27:50.650 --> 00:27:55.930
<v Speaker 1>This necessity for constant, unyielding practice is captured perfectly in the instruction from the prophet.

488
00:27:56.010 --> 00:27:57.210
<v Speaker 1>A man approached him one day.

489
00:27:57.930 --> 00:28:03.850
<v Speaker 1>This man was overwhelmed by the sheer number of religious duties, the complex laws, the myriad requirements of the faith.

490
00:28:03.850 --> 00:28:05.290
<v Speaker 2>He was drowning in the details.

491
00:28:05.290 --> 00:28:08.490
<v Speaker 1>He felt burdened, exhausted by the effort of trying to keep track of it all.

492
00:28:08.730 --> 00:28:14.330
<v Speaker 1>He asked the prophet for a single comprehensive practice, one core thing he could hold onto to anchor his soul.

493
00:28:14.330 --> 00:28:15.530
<v Speaker 2>And what did the prophet give him?

494
00:28:15.970 --> 00:28:18.890
<v Speaker 1>He gave him a remarkably physical, immediate remedy.

495
00:28:18.890 --> 00:28:23.450
<v Speaker 1>He told the man, let your tongue never cease to be moist with the remembrance of God.

496
00:28:23.610 --> 00:28:26.490
<v Speaker 2>The physicality of that instruction is absolutely striking.

497
00:28:26.730 --> 00:28:28.210
<v Speaker 2>It is a perpetual moistening.

498
00:28:28.210 --> 00:28:29.770
<v Speaker 1>It isn't just a mental exercise.

499
00:28:29.770 --> 00:28:30.650
<v Speaker 2>No, not at all.

500
00:28:30.850 --> 00:28:33.930
<v Speaker 2>The prophet didn't say, let your mind dwell on high thoughts.

501
00:28:34.330 --> 00:28:37.770
<v Speaker 2>He didn't say, engage in abstract silent meditation.

502
00:28:38.010 --> 00:28:39.210
<v Speaker 2>He pointed to the tongue.

503
00:28:39.690 --> 00:28:43.850
<v Speaker 2>He pointed to saliva, to the vocal cords, to the physical movement of the body.

504
00:28:44.250 --> 00:28:45.890
<v Speaker 2>It is an ongoing bodily devotion.

505
00:28:46.250 --> 00:28:47.290
<v Speaker 2>to the name of God.

506
00:28:47.450 --> 00:28:50.170
<v Speaker 1>It grounds the infinite in the physical body.

507
00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:55.850
<v Speaker 2>Yes, the physical act of moving the tongue, the breath carrying the name, the subtle vibration in the chest.

508
00:28:56.370 --> 00:29:01.450
<v Speaker 2>This physical action becomes the constant counter friction to the constant rusting of the heart.

509
00:29:01.570 --> 00:29:02.730
<v Speaker 1>The medicine in motion.

510
00:29:02.890 --> 00:29:08.890
<v Speaker 2>The name of God is itself the cure, and taking the cure requires the body's active, continuous participation.

511
00:29:09.210 --> 00:29:16.410
<v Speaker 1>But, you know, introducing this continuous, formless practice often raises a significant defense from the structured, rational mind.

512
00:29:16.730 --> 00:29:21.130
<v Speaker 1>The ego is very clever, and it will use even spiritual concepts to avoid hard work.

513
00:29:21.370 --> 00:29:23.450
<v Speaker 2>I can hear the objection forming already.

514
00:29:24.010 --> 00:29:40.730
<v Speaker 2>If this remembrance is so continuous and formless, if it is something I can do while standing, while sitting, while walking through the noisy marketplace, while doing my daily chores, isn't that just a very convenient excuse to abandon the difficult, rigid discipline of formal ritual prayer?

515
00:29:40.810 --> 00:29:42.570
<v Speaker 1>That's the loophole people look for.

516
00:29:42.570 --> 00:29:48.650
<v Speaker 2>It is very easy, almost seductive, for someone to say, look, my tongue is moist with remembrance.

517
00:29:48.810 --> 00:29:53.890
<v Speaker 2>I am remembering God in my heart while I go about my day, so I don't need the rigid constraints

518
00:29:54.010 --> 00:29:55.450
<v Speaker 2>of specific prayer times.

519
00:29:55.610 --> 00:29:58.730
<v Speaker 2>I don't need to perform the physical bows and prostrations.

520
00:29:58.970 --> 00:30:00.890
<v Speaker 2>My whole life is a prayer.

521
00:30:01.130 --> 00:30:05.130
<v Speaker 1>That is exactly how the ego attempts to co-opt the practice.

522
00:30:05.610 --> 00:30:09.210
<v Speaker 1>It uses the boundlessness of the remembrance to escape the discipline of the law.

523
00:30:09.930 --> 00:30:15.770
<v Speaker 1>But the scripture defines the relationship between the two very carefully, and they are not in competition.

524
00:30:16.250 --> 00:30:16.890
<v Speaker 2>They work together.

525
00:30:16.890 --> 00:30:24.330
<v Speaker 1>The Quran states in chapter 29, verse 45, that formal prayer keeps one from iniquity, but the remembrance of God is greater.

526
00:30:24.410 --> 00:30:24.890
<v Speaker 2>Greater.

527
00:30:24.890 --> 00:30:28.970
<v Speaker 2>We have to unpack what greater means here, because it doesn't mean a replacement for.

528
00:30:29.210 --> 00:30:29.970
<v Speaker 1>No, it does not.

529
00:30:30.810 --> 00:30:33.290
<v Speaker 1>Formal prayer acts as a vital preventative.

530
00:30:33.810 --> 00:30:36.570
<v Speaker 1>It fences the soul against its worst impulses.

531
00:30:36.810 --> 00:30:40.170
<v Speaker 1>It provides an indispensable architectural structure to the day.

532
00:30:40.650 --> 00:30:42.330
<v Speaker 1>It breaks up the monotony of time.

533
00:30:42.330 --> 00:30:42.970
<v Speaker 2>A framework.

534
00:30:43.130 --> 00:30:46.330
<v Speaker 1>But a fence is, by definition, a limitation.

535
00:30:46.730 --> 00:30:48.210
<v Speaker 1>It happens at specific times.

536
00:30:48.210 --> 00:30:49.650
<v Speaker 1>You face a specific direction.

537
00:30:49.650 --> 00:30:51.930
<v Speaker 1>You say specific mandated words.

538
00:30:52.410 --> 00:30:53.530
<v Speaker 1>It is bounded.

539
00:30:53.850 --> 00:30:58.010
<v Speaker 2>And because it is bounded, if that is the only thing a person relies on,

540
00:30:58.370 --> 00:31:00.730
<v Speaker 2>Their connection to God becomes bounded as well.

541
00:31:00.810 --> 00:31:03.450
<v Speaker 1>That is the danger of relying solely on the form.

542
00:31:03.450 --> 00:31:07.850
<v Speaker 2>They feel near to God only when they're on the prayer mat, standing in a sacred space.

543
00:31:08.250 --> 00:31:15.770
<v Speaker 2>And the moment they step off that mat, the moment they walk back into the noise of the market or the stress of their home, they are back in the secular, opaque world.

544
00:31:16.170 --> 00:31:20.010
<v Speaker 2>The fence protects the garden, but it does not permeate the rest of the landscape.

545
00:31:20.010 --> 00:31:20.730
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

546
00:31:20.730 --> 00:31:23.610
<v Speaker 1>The formal prayer is the vessel, but what goes inside it.

547
00:31:23.890 --> 00:31:26.010
<v Speaker 1>This is why the limitless practice is required.

548
00:31:26.330 --> 00:31:30.210
<v Speaker 1>The fifth imam noted that everything in religious law has a limit at which it stops.

549
00:31:30.210 --> 00:31:31.770
<v Speaker 2>Fastening ends when the sun goes down.

550
00:31:31.930 --> 00:31:32.490
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

551
00:31:32.970 --> 00:31:35.770
<v Speaker 1>Prescribed duties finish when their conditions are met.

552
00:31:36.170 --> 00:31:38.010
<v Speaker 1>Charity has a calculated amount.

553
00:31:38.490 --> 00:31:43.930
<v Speaker 1>Every formal requirement has a boundary, an endpoint, except one thing.

554
00:31:44.530 --> 00:31:45.610
<v Speaker 2>The remembrance of God.

555
00:31:45.610 --> 00:31:46.890
<v Speaker 1>The remembrance of God.

556
00:31:46.890 --> 00:31:47.890
<v Speaker 1>It has no boundary.

557
00:31:47.890 --> 00:31:48.850
<v Speaker 1>It has no time limit.

558
00:31:48.850 --> 00:31:50.650
<v Speaker 1>It has no designated location.

559
00:31:51.050 --> 00:31:54.250
<v Speaker 1>The remembrance does not abolish the formal prayer.

560
00:31:54.570 --> 00:31:58.490
<v Speaker 1>It is the greater substance that pours into the vessel of formal prayer.

561
00:31:58.490 --> 00:31:59.610
<v Speaker 2>And then it overpours.

562
00:31:59.690 --> 00:32:05.130
<v Speaker 1>And then, because it is boundless, it spills out of that vessel to fill every ordinary hour of life.

563
00:32:05.210 --> 00:32:06.770
<v Speaker 2>The lesser is not left behind.

564
00:32:06.890 --> 00:32:08.410
<v Speaker 2>It is completed by the greater.

565
00:32:08.410 --> 00:32:09.210
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

566
00:32:09.450 --> 00:32:14.010
<v Speaker 1>The continuous invocation ensures that the intense awareness generated inside

567
00:32:14.170 --> 00:32:15.610
<v Speaker 1>formal prayer is carried over.

568
00:32:15.930 --> 00:32:19.610
<v Speaker 1>It is carried into the marketplace, into the home, into the silence of the night.

569
00:32:19.930 --> 00:32:25.810
<v Speaker 1>It is the thread that strings the pearls of formal prayer together so the day becomes a single necklace of presence.

570
00:32:25.810 --> 00:32:35.130
<v Speaker 2>So the continuous practice, the moist tongue, the constant polishing of the blade, this is the physical mechanism that allows a human being to maintain the vision of the veil that reveals

571
00:32:35.530 --> 00:32:38.170
<v Speaker 2>rather than falling back into the nightmare of the veil that hides.

572
00:32:38.170 --> 00:32:39.130
<v Speaker 1>It is the only way.

573
00:32:39.290 --> 00:32:42.570
<v Speaker 2>The polished heart is maintained entirely by the moist tongue.

574
00:32:42.730 --> 00:32:44.570
<v Speaker 1>And this brings us to the radical turn.

575
00:32:44.730 --> 00:32:49.450
<v Speaker 1>This is the final and undoubtedly the most vertiginous teaching of the Imam.

576
00:32:50.010 --> 00:32:53.570
<v Speaker 1>It is a teaching that forces us to look entirely past ourselves.

577
00:32:53.570 --> 00:32:54.970
<v Speaker 2>Which is the hardest thing of all.

578
00:32:55.050 --> 00:33:03.210
<v Speaker 1>Everything we have discussed so far, the inner interpretation, tracing the light to the source, the careful balance of immanence and transcendence,

579
00:33:03.570 --> 00:33:09.530
<v Speaker 1>The continual moistening of the tongue to polish the rusting heart, all of it assumes one very basic thing.

580
00:33:09.850 --> 00:33:12.490
<v Speaker 2>It assumes there is a self who is doing the invoking.

581
00:33:12.570 --> 00:33:13.050
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

582
00:33:13.210 --> 00:33:20.090
<v Speaker 1>It assumes there is a person, an individual ego, a distinct entity holding the polish and applying it to their own heart.

583
00:33:20.330 --> 00:33:23.450
<v Speaker 2>And that is the baseline of our entire lived experience.

584
00:33:24.010 --> 00:33:26.810
<v Speaker 2>When I invoke the name, I am the one remembering.

585
00:33:26.890 --> 00:33:28.410
<v Speaker 2>I am the reader looking at the ink.

586
00:33:28.730 --> 00:33:30.490
<v Speaker 2>I am the observer watching the light.

587
00:33:31.170 --> 00:33:33.770
<v Speaker 2>There's God and there is me trying to remember him.

588
00:33:33.850 --> 00:33:36.770
<v Speaker 1>But the imam dismantles even that baseline assumption.

589
00:33:36.770 --> 00:33:38.410
<v Speaker 2>He really tears down the final wall.

590
00:33:38.410 --> 00:33:40.330
<v Speaker 1>He strips it all the way down to the studs.

591
00:33:40.570 --> 00:33:45.210
<v Speaker 1>He says that one does not truly remember God until one forgets one's own soul in the remembrance.

592
00:33:45.370 --> 00:33:46.930
<v Speaker 2>Let that sink in for a moment.

593
00:33:47.210 --> 00:33:51.290
<v Speaker 2>Forgetting one's own soul, that is the ultimate effacement of the self.

594
00:33:51.290 --> 00:33:53.210
<v Speaker 1>It is absolute extinction.

595
00:33:53.450 --> 00:34:00.650
<v Speaker 1>At the summit of this practice, the individual who set out so earnestly to remember God realizes A terrifying and beautiful truth.

596
00:34:01.530 --> 00:34:07.370
<v Speaker 1>Their own separate ego, their sense of me doing the remembering was the final, most stubborn veil.

597
00:34:07.690 --> 00:34:16.330
<v Speaker 2>The polished mirror becomes so incredibly clear, so perfectly devoid of rust or distortion, that the glass itself disappears from view.

598
00:34:16.970 --> 00:34:19.450
<v Speaker 2>You no longer see the mirror, you only see the reflection.

599
00:34:19.530 --> 00:34:24.090
<v Speaker 1>Perfect remembrance requires the total extinction of the individual who is doing the remembering.

600
00:34:24.370 --> 00:34:27.890
<v Speaker 1>At the absolute peak of invocation, the one who invoked vanishes.

601
00:34:27.890 --> 00:34:29.050
<v Speaker 2>It's all burned away.

602
00:34:29.210 --> 00:34:33.050
<v Speaker 1>The illusion of an independent, separate self is burned away in the fire of the name.

603
00:34:33.050 --> 00:34:34.250
<v Speaker 1>Only the name remains.

604
00:34:34.410 --> 00:34:37.370
<v Speaker 2>It is the ultimate confirmation of our entire thesis.

605
00:34:37.450 --> 00:34:44.490
<v Speaker 2>When the self is effaced, when the heavy demanding ego is finally laid down, we arrive exactly where we began, but with total clarity.

606
00:34:44.810 --> 00:34:46.370
<v Speaker 2>We see the world as it truly is.

607
00:34:46.570 --> 00:34:47.610
<v Speaker 1>And what is that truth?

608
00:34:47.930 --> 00:34:51.610
<v Speaker 1>We state it again, for the final time, the spine of the entire vision.

609
00:34:51.850 --> 00:34:53.530
<v Speaker 1>The veils do not veil him.

610
00:34:53.690 --> 00:34:54.810
<v Speaker 2>The world remains.

611
00:34:55.050 --> 00:34:56.330
<v Speaker 2>The trees are still trees.

612
00:34:56.410 --> 00:34:57.770
<v Speaker 2>The ink is still on the page.

613
00:34:58.010 --> 00:35:00.410
<v Speaker 2>The individual physical forms still walks the earth.

614
00:35:00.890 --> 00:35:04.010
<v Speaker 2>But it is no longer misread as a solid, opaque wall.

615
00:35:04.650 --> 00:35:05.690
<v Speaker 2>The rust is gone.

616
00:35:06.250 --> 00:35:08.970
<v Speaker 2>The creation is entirely transparent to the source.

617
00:35:09.650 --> 00:35:16.250
<v Speaker 1>And so we turn directly to you, the listener, with an unresolved challenge to carry forward into the reality of your own week.

618
00:35:16.250 --> 00:35:17.210
<v Speaker 1>This is not just theory.

619
00:35:17.210 --> 00:35:26.730
<v Speaker 1>This is about how you perceive the weight of your own life, which surface in your own life, which frustrating task, which difficult person, which physical exhaustion are you still mistaking for the whole of things?

620
00:35:27.130 --> 00:35:32.490
<v Speaker 1>And what would beginning the small continual remembrance actually cost you this week?
